---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    expert: 'Mike Wallach',
    handle: '@mike_on_parenting',
    title: 'The more things you take away, the more things you have to take away',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfGsJpbRWdJ/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Taking things away as the primary strategy creates a cycle: threaten, remove, look for the next thing to take away — without the behavior improving. Shift to what you want to see and what the child gets to experience when they do it: "When I see you helping without me having to ask… you might get bonus time later," "When I see you giving your sister a turn without her having to ask, she\'s gonna want to be nicer to you," "When your homework gets done before dinner, we\'ll have time to play a game later." Boundaries and consequences still have a place — just not as the main lever.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Mike Wallach',
    handle: '@mike_on_parenting',
    title: 'You took away all their toys — and they still keep fighting?',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYVLKopyx6A/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Wallach\'s test for any discipline strategy is one question: did it actually change the behavior moving forward? If the behavior keeps happening, the method was ineffective — and repeating it louder isn\'t a new strategy. Timeouts, toy removal, lectures, and threats all fail the same test, he argues, because kids don\'t magically learn new skills just because a parent is frustrated. The shift: more connection, more teaching, more consistency, less reacting on repeat — "You don\'t need harsher consequences. You need strategies that actually build the behaviors you want to see more of."',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: '@drbeckyatgoodinside',
    title: 'Punishment creates shame — and shame blocks learning',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DFGNxFjPWzx/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Punishments separate a child further from the identity you know they have — that they\'re a good kid — and that separation produces shame and disconnection, which are two huge blockers to learning. And learning is exactly what a child needs to do to stop the behavior. The reel points to her "Effective Alternatives to Punishments" workshop for the fuller framework, but the core claim stands alone: punishment doesn\'t teach, because a shamed, disconnected brain can\'t learn. (The comments even ask the question this whole topic turns on: "What if an iPad is taken away for a couple of days… Is it an acceptable strategy?")',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Siggie Cohen',
    handle: '@dr.siggie',
    title: 'Punishment vs. discipline: the iPad test',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/De9oglpP2vV/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Discipline is essential — it\'s tied to motivation, resilience, regulation, and functioning — but discipline is not punishment; it\'s teaching, guiding, mentoring. Her iPad contrast makes it concrete: punishment sounds like "That\'s it! No iPad for the rest of the week because you can\'t listen," which tells the child "you did something wrong" and makes them pay in the moment. Discipline starts with empathy for the hard moment ("I see you\'re having a hard time stopping") and teaches the skill instead of collecting the price.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Devon Kuntzman',
    handle: '@transformingtoddlerhood',
    title: 'Consequences don\'t work? Decisive, no drama',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DJAmdcXPueC/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Kuntzman\'s headline is blunt — consequences are largely ineffective at changing behavior, especially with strong-willed kids who prize agency — but that\'s not the same as having no boundaries. His alternative starts with connection before correction: connect so you have the child\'s attention, because connection creates influence and influence creates cooperation. Then use shaping — reinforcing small, manageable steps toward the bigger goal, like praising one toy picked up before expecting the whole room cleaned. Discipline, in this frame, is decisive and drama-free, but it teaches rather than extracts a cost.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Kirk Martin · Calm Parenting Podcast',
    handle: '@calmparentingpodcast',
    title: 'Punishment removes; discipline adds',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DTUGGMNjAhp/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Kirk Martin draws the line as removal versus addition: punishment is the removal of privileges or objects, discipline is the addition of tools, skills, and responsibility. Punishment stops an unwanted behavior temporarily; discipline equips the child to avoid repeating the mistake. His headline claim: you cannot demand respect, you must demonstrate self-respect — and his episode #551 ("Which Consequences Work for Lying, Disrespect & Defiance?") is where he works through which consequences actually teach.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Big Little Feelings',
    handle: '@biglittlefeelings',
    title: 'Consequences have to be related',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DOb06mskrmb/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Sending a child away doesn\'t teach them how to do better next time — teaching does. BLF are explicit that they do use consequences, but only related ones: "Your toddler throws a toy + the toy goes away" — simple, immediate, and easy for a toddler to follow. Isolation during a hard moment, by contrast, teaches a child that when they\'re having a hard time they should be alone. The rule: the consequence has to be connected to the behavior, or the child can\'t connect the dots.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Big Little Feelings',
    handle: '@biglittlefeelings',
    title: 'Mean what you say',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DFiZFqQOARx/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Dinnertime, everyone\'s exhausted, the kid is still on the iPad after being asked to put it away — and the parent asks again, and again. BLF\'s point is that kids stop listening the first time because parents keep issuing consequences they never follow through on: "If you don\'t put your shoes on, we\'re not going to school" (they still go), "we are going to land this airplane" (the plane doesn\'t land). Empty threats teach children that limits are negotiable, so the fix is to say only what you\'ll actually do — and then do it.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Mona · PedsDoctalk',
    handle: '@pedsdoctalk',
    title: 'When natural consequences work — and when they don\'t',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ7lazuEq1V/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Natural consequences can be helpful teachers, but they\'re not magic and not a free pass to step back and hope the lesson lands — the current "FAFO parenting" talk gets this wrong. Young kids don\'t always connect the dots on their own: they\'re impulsive, curious, and still learning cause and effect, so the moment matters but the parent\'s support matters too. A spilled cup, a forgotten backpack, a toy left out in the rain are all opportunities — but the lesson comes from the experience plus the parent\'s help, not from shame, blame, or leaving a child alone in frustration.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Willough Jenkins',
    handle: '@drwilloughjenkins',
    title: 'Just take away their phone? It\'s not that simple',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DU1v5R1ErJT/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'Taking a teen\'s phone away is often the fastest consequence, but fast doesn\'t mean effective — it\'s the default punishment for everything: late homework, attitude, forgotten chores. The problem is fit: when the consequence isn\'t related to the behavior it feels unfair, and unfair doesn\'t teach, it creates power struggles. Worse, for teens the phone isn\'t just entertainment, it\'s their social world, their friends, their support, sometimes their lifeline — so removing it can accidentally create isolation right when connection matters most. Her test before disciplining: is the consequence connected to the behavior, and does it teach a skill or just create resentment? Clear rules agreed ahead of time beat heat-of-the-moment punishments.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Deborah Tillman',
    handle: '@drdeborahpurposedparenting',
    title: 'A threat is not a consequence',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaoboEWiWvQ/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'A screaming child is told "Do you want me to spank you?" and screams again — the threat changed nothing, because the child has learned the threat is where it ends. Tillman\'s fix is to set the rule before the moment ("If you want something, ask with a calm voice. If you scream, the answer is no") and then, the second they scream, say one sentence — "The answer is no because you screamed" — and stop talking. No repeated warnings, no raised voice; the answer stays no until they ask calmly, so screaming stops working and asking starts working. Her throughline: predictable consequences change behavior; threats don\'t.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Deborah Tillman',
    handle: '@drdeborahpurposedparenting',
    title: 'Homework starts now. Roblox comes after.',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZWIiUcACg1/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'When a seven-year-old argues every time homework starts, most parents explain why homework matters, repeat the direction, raise their voice, or get pulled into proving they\'re right — which teaches the child that arguing delays the work. Tillman\'s script, said once: "Homework starts now. Roblox comes after homework. If you argue, homework is still now, and Roblox is off today." The second they argue, Roblox is off — no debate, no lecture, no second chance, and it doesn\'t come back just because they suddenly say "Fine, I\'ll do it." One afternoon of disappointment, she argues, is cheaper than a system where arguing always buys a delay.',
  },
];
---
<Layout title="Consequences That Actually Teach — Parenting" description="Mike Wallach, Dr. Becky Kennedy, Dr. Siggie, Dr. Deborah Tillman and more, summarized from their Instagram reels: why taking things away stops working, what discipline means if it isn't punishment, and scripts you can use.">
  <main class="bg-[#0c0b09] min-h-screen px-4 py-14 sm:py-20 text-[#ece5d3]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/parenting" class="text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline">← All parenting topics</a>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-[#c9a86a]">Topic 03 · Discipline</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight leading-[1.05] text-[#f7f0dc]">Consequences that actually teach</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        Summarized from <strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">12 Instagram reels</strong> by Mike Wallach, Dr. Becky Kennedy,
        Dr. Siggie Cohen, Devon Kuntzman, Kirk Martin, Big Little Feelings, Dr. Mona, Dr. Willough Jenkins,
        and Dr. Deborah Tillman. Every summary below links to the original — watch the reel, then use the script.
      </p>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#c9a86a]/40 bg-[#c9a86a]/10 p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">The short answer</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xl leading-relaxed text-[#f7f0dc] font-medium">Taking things away as your main strategy doesn't teach — it just trains your child to wait out the next threat. Discipline that works teaches a skill instead of collecting a price.</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The test:</strong> did the behavior actually change? If it keeps happening, the method failed — repeating it louder isn't a new strategy. (Wallach)</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">What replaces it:</strong> say what you want to see, and what your child gains by doing it. Connect before you correct. Shape small steps, don't demand the whole room cleaned at once.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">When consequences do work:</strong> related to the behavior, immediate, said once, and actually followed through. Agreed ahead of time, not invented in the heat of the moment.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The split:</strong> the connection-first camp says punishment creates shame and shame blocks learning; the consequence-forward camp says predictable consequences change behavior and threats don't. Both sides agree a threat is not a consequence.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Why taking things away stops working -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Why taking things away stops working</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. The threat cycle — Wallach.</strong> "If you do that one more time, I'm taking it away." "If you don't stop, you're losing screen time." Before long you're constantly threatening consequences, removing privileges, and hunting for the next thing to take away — and the behavior is still there. The method's own test exposes it: if the behavior keeps happening, the method was ineffective.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. Shame blocks learning — Dr. Becky.</strong> Punishment separates a child further from the identity you know they have — that they're a good kid. That separation produces shame and disconnection, and a shamed, disconnected brain can't learn. Learning is exactly what a child needs to do to stop the behavior, so the punishment defeats its own purpose.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. Empty threats teach negotiability — Big Little Feelings.</strong> "If you don't put your shoes on, we're not going to school" — and then you go. "We are going to land this airplane" — and the plane doesn't land. Kids stop listening the first time because parents keep issuing consequences they never follow through on. Every unkept threat teaches the child that limits are negotiable.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. Wrong fit creates power struggles — Dr. Jenkins.</strong> The phone is the default punishment for everything: late homework, attitude, forgotten chores. But when the consequence isn't related to the behavior it feels unfair, and unfair doesn't teach — it creates power struggles. For teens it's worse: the phone is their social world and support, so removing it can isolate them exactly when connection matters most.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">5. Punishment removes; discipline adds — Kirk Martin.</strong> Punishment is the removal of privileges or objects. Discipline is the addition of tools, skills, and responsibility. One stops an unwanted behavior temporarily; the other equips the child to avoid repeating the mistake. You cannot demand respect, Martin says — you must demonstrate self-respect.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- What to do instead -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">What to do instead: the playbook</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88] leading-relaxed">Synthesized from all nine experts. They disagree on consequences — see below — but they converge on the order of operations.</p>
        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">1 · Connect before you correct.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Kuntzman and Siggie start here: connect so you have the child's attention, because connection creates influence and influence creates cooperation. Siggie's version opens with empathy for the hard moment — "I see you're having a hard time stopping" — before any teaching begins.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">2 · Say what you want to see — and what it earns.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Wallach's shift: give your child something to work toward instead of something to lose. "When I see you helping without me having to ask… you might get bonus time later." The child experiences the positive side of the behavior, not just the threat of losing it.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">3 · Shape small steps.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Kuntzman's shaping: reinforce small, manageable steps toward the bigger goal — praise one toy picked up before expecting the whole room cleaned. Decisive, no drama, but the discipline teaches rather than extracts a cost.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">4 · If there's a consequence, make it related and immediate.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">The one rule nearly everyone lands on: the consequence has to be connected to the behavior or the child can't connect the dots. Toy thrown → toy goes away. Simple, immediate, easy for a toddler to follow. (BLF, Siggie, Jenkins, PedsDoctalk.)</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">5 · Say it once, mean it, and stop talking.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">BLF: say only what you'll actually do, then do it. Tillman: set the rule before the moment, then one sentence when it happens — "The answer is no because you screamed" — and stop. Repeated warnings and lectures teach the child that the first five times don't count.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">6 · Let natural consequences teach — with you beside them.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">PedsDoctalk: a spilled cup, a forgotten backpack, a toy left in the rain are real teachers — but young kids don't always connect the dots alone, so the lesson comes from the experience plus the parent's help, not from shame, blame, or leaving a child alone in frustration.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">7 · Agree the rules ahead of time.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Jenkins: clear rules agreed before the moment beat heat-of-the-moment punishments every time. Tillman agrees from the other side — set the rule before the moment, so the consequence is predictable rather than invented in anger.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- Scripts -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Scripts you can copy</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Instead of "I'm taking it away"</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"When I see you helping without me having to ask… you might get bonus time later." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Wallach — give them something to work toward.)</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">The sister turn</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"When I see you giving your sister a turn without her having to ask, she's gonna want to be nicer to you." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Wallach — the payoff is in the relationship, not the parent's approval.)</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Homework, said once</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"Homework starts now. Roblox comes after homework. If you argue, homework is still now, and Roblox is off today." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Tillman — then stop talking. No debate, no lecture, no second chance.)</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Screaming for something</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"The answer is no because you screamed." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Tillman — one sentence, then stop. The answer stays no until they ask calmly.)</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">The iPad, discipline version</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"I see you're having a hard time stopping." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Siggie — empathy first, then teach the skill. The punishment version is "That's it! No iPad for the rest of the week because you can't listen.")</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">The related consequence</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"Your toddler throws a toy + the toy goes away." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(BLF — simple, immediate, connected. Not a week without the iPad for a thrown block.)</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Disagreements -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Where the experts disagree</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88] leading-relaxed">In their own words. No verdict here — the disagreement is the point.</p>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Do consequences change behavior at all?</strong> Tillman: predictable consequences change behavior — "the answer stays no until they ask calmly, so screaming stops working and asking starts working," and one afternoon of disappointment is cheaper than a system where arguing always buys a delay. Wallach and Kuntzman: consequences are largely ineffective at changing behavior, especially with strong-willed kids — "if the behavior keeps happening, the method was ineffective," and kids don't learn new skills just because a parent is frustrated.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Punishment vs. discipline:</strong> Dr. Becky: punishment separates the child from the identity that they're a good kid, and the shame and disconnection that follow block learning. Siggie: discipline is teaching, guiding, mentoring — "it teaches the skill instead of collecting the price." Kirk Martin: "punishment is the removal of privileges or objects, discipline is the addition of tools, skills, and responsibility." Tillman, from the other side, treats a fast, predictable consequence as the discipline itself — the teaching is in the predictability.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Natural consequences — hands off or hands on?</strong> PedsDoctalk: natural consequences can teach, but young kids don't always connect the dots alone — the lesson comes from the experience plus the parent's help, not from stepping back and hoping. The "FAFO parenting" framing she pushes back on says the opposite: let the consequence do the teaching and stay out of it.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">How much should the consequence cost?</strong> BLF and Jenkins: it has to be related and proportionate — a thrown toy loses the toy, not the whole week. Tillman's Roblox rule is the other pole: argue once and Roblox is off for the whole day, no second chance, because a small cost that returns on demand teaches the child that arguing works.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- When to get support -->
      <section class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.04] p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">When it's more than a strategy problem</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">If you're taking things away every day and nothing is changing, the issue probably isn't the size of the consequence — it's the fit. Same if discipline in your house is mostly threats, or if a child seems to live in fear of the next punishment rather than learning from the last one. A pediatrician, child psychologist, or parenting coach can help sort out whether the behavior needs a different skill, a different setup, or a different kind of support. The reels above are summaries, not a substitute for that conversation.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Sources -->
      <section class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The sources, summarized one by one</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88]">Everything above is drawn from these Instagram reels. Summaries are in our words; the originals are the experts'.</p>
        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5">
          {sources.map((s, i) => (
            <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
                <span class="text-[#c9a86a] font-bold">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">{s.title}</h3>
                <span class="text-xs rounded-full border border-white/15 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.format}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">{s.expert} · {s.handle}</p>
              <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.summary}</p>
              <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-3 inline-block text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline break-all">Watch on Instagram ↗ {s.url}</a>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[#8a8474]">Compiled 1 November 2026.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">This page summarizes public social-media posts for personal reference. It is not medical or psychological advice.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
